Boudewijn Wayers (dedos4@wsintt05.info.win.tue.nl) wrote:
: The alignments are there to balance the game and to put more
: distinction between character classes. A knight, for example, is not
: only lawful, but he even has the chivalry code that he must stick to.
: This would be totally illogical for a chaotic knight. If the alignment
: was good/evil, you would have a point, but it's lawful/chaotic. The
: original poster mentioned elves, that he couldn't imagine as "evil",
: but the elves in Tolkien certainly weren't lawful! I'd sooner call them
: chaotic good, chaotic neutral, or even, in some cases, neutral evil.

This is a moot point.  You are interpreting D&D alignments in
an AD&D way.  Chaotic, in D&D, means, for all practical purposes,
evil.  Lawful means good.  This is a gross oversimplification, 
but so is the current alignment system.  When there is no other
measure of alignment than lawful and chaotic, then that's what
they usually mean.  Until NetHack adds good and evil to law and
chaos, for the AD&D 9point alignment system (or something better,
AD&D alignments are silly, IMHO), only evil things should be
chaotic and only good should be lawful.

In summary, In D&D (and NetHack), chaotic and lawful are the
whole of the alignment and mean one thing, while in AD&D, they
have different meanings, because good and evil are added as an
extra axis.  Thus, the game makes elves evil, even though this
is, IMHO, a gross misrepresentation. 

As a final thought, orcs are also chaotic.  I've never played
an elf (in recent memory, anyway, I play priests because I'm
not good enough to get by without the easy blessed/cursed ID
of the priest), but wouldn't orcs be peaceful to elves if both
are chaotic?  I seem to recall peaceful goblins for my chaotic
priests, anyway.

: ANyway, if you let a player choose for himself, there is no reason why
: not everyone would choose to be neutral. If you are neutral, you can
: become (with a helmet of opposite alignment) both lawful and chaotic,
: so you can have ALL the advantages. If you start L/E, you are
: restricted to those two, save for converting yourself to neutral.

Make a helm of opposite alignment have no effect on neutrals,
or make them unaligned (convert them to Moloch?).  This would
drastically reduce the benefits.  In any case, as things now
stand, you are entirely right, neutral is the best long-term
alignment.

: I think alignments should stay as they are now. They add spice to the
: game. You might as well have questions at game start whether you want
: the intrinsic gem identify of the archeologist, the automatic curse
: detection of the priest, etc. The fact that you cannot choose restricts
: you a bit, and that's something that is badly needed in a game like
: NetHack where you can do almost everything.

I disagree.  Chaotics are grossly crippled, and I rarely act
chaotic (slaying peacefuls etc.) anyway, so why should I have
to stick with an alignment that gives *ME* no advantages and a
whole passel of disadvantages.  Sure, I can convert, but it is
a real pain in the neck.

Since it is possible to have a priest who is not chaotic, I 
can see no reason why I should have to start that way.  If the
alignment can vary, let people choose.  But you don't have to 
add choices to classes that don't have different starers already.

